WEBVTT

00:00:00.192 --> 00:00:04.480
In self-attention, queries, keys and
values all come from one set.

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:09.017
Cross-attention takes the queries from one
set and the keys and values from another.

00:00:09.017 --> 00:00:11.548
DETR uses one hundred learned object
queries,

00:00:11.549 --> 00:00:13.855
and eight hundred and fifty image tokens.

00:00:13.854 --> 00:00:17.512
Each query reads from the whole image and
comes back with one row,

00:00:17.513 --> 00:00:19.230
however many tokens there were.

00:00:19.229 --> 00:00:21.267
Here a query scores three tokens:

00:00:21.267 --> 00:00:23.180
four, zero and two.

00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:26.167
After softmax, it takes mostly from the
first.

00:00:26.165 --> 00:00:30.977
Attention alone treats its tokens as a
set, so positions must be added.

00:00:30.977 --> 00:00:35.527
Sinusoidal encodings give each position a
point on several unit circles.

00:00:35.527 --> 00:00:38.078
A fast circle separates neighbours.

00:00:38.078 --> 00:00:40.890
A slow one tells distant positions apart.

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:44.362
And moving by a fixed offset just rotates
every pair,

00:00:44.363 --> 00:00:48.491
so the dot product of two encodings
depends only on the offset.

00:00:48.413 --> 00:00:53.063
DETR outputs one hundred predictions, but
this image has two objects.

00:00:53.063 --> 00:00:55.813
And the objects come in no particular
order.

00:00:55.813 --> 00:00:58.726
So which prediction should be blamed for
which object?

00:00:58.726 --> 00:01:01.313
Pause and think about how you would
decide.

00:01:02.781 --> 00:01:06.194
Choose the best one-to-one assignment, and
then compute the loss.

00:01:06.194 --> 00:01:09.581
Build a cost matrix from class scores and
box overlap.

00:01:09.581 --> 00:01:12.069
The Hungarian algorithm picks the minimum:

00:01:12.069 --> 00:01:16.819
cup to prediction one, spoon to prediction
three, a total of minus one.

00:01:16.819 --> 00:01:21.184
Prediction two is a near-duplicate of one,
so it's matched to no object,

00:01:21.185 --> 00:01:22.882
and its loss pushes it down.

00:01:22.881 --> 00:01:27.631
Duplicates are trained away, which is why
DETR needs no non-maximum suppression.

00:01:27.569 --> 00:01:28.982
One last problem.

00:01:28.982 --> 00:01:32.851
Two separated boxes have an IoU of zero
wherever they are,

00:01:32.852 --> 00:01:34.920
so the loss gives no direction.

00:01:34.919 --> 00:01:39.857
Generalised IoU subtracts the share of the
enclosing box that neither covers.

00:01:39.857 --> 00:01:42.982
Slide the box closer and the loss falls
steadily:

00:01:42.982 --> 00:01:47.432
one point five, one point two, one, and
point six seven.

00:01:47.432 --> 00:01:49.719
The boxes now know which way to move.
